=== Comet flyby ===
[[File:Animation of Deep Impact trajectory.gif|thumb|right|Animation of Deep Impact{{'s}} trajectory from 12 January 2005 to 8 August 2013<br> {{legend2|magenta| Deep Impact 1}} {{legend2|lime|[[Tempel 1]]}} {{legend2|royalblue|[[Earth]]}} {{legend2|cyan|[[103P/Hartley]]}}]]
{{see also|103P/Hartley}}
{{see also|Tempel 1}}
[[File:Introducing Comet Hartley 2.png|thumb|The nucleus of comet 103P/Hartley measuring approximately 2 kilometers  in length and .4 kilometers at its most narrow portion or neck. Jets can be seen streaming out of the nucleus.]]
The spacecraft used Earth's gravity for the second gravity assist in December 2008 and made two distant flybys of Earth in June and December 2009. On May 30, 2010 it successfully fired its engines for an 11.3 second trajectory correction maneuver, for a velocity change (Δv) of {{convert|0.1|m/s|mph|sp=us}}, in preparation for the third Earth flyby on June 27. Observations of 103P/Hartley began on September 5 and ended November 25, 2010.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/epoxi/epoxi20100528.html |title=NASA Spacecraft Burns for Home, Then Comet |publisher=NASA |access-date=2010-06-01 |archive-date=2021-04-08 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210408085117/https://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/epoxi/epoxi20100528.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> For a diagram of the EPOXI solar orbits see [https://web.archive.org/web/20100905134548/http://epoxi.umd.edu/3gallery/DIXI_path_full.shtml here].
[[File:495452main epoxi-3-4x3 946-710.jpg|thumb|left|Another view of the comet, taken near closest approach.]]
The mission's closest approach to 103P/Hartley occurred at 10 am EDT on 4 November 2010, passing to within {{convert|694|km|mi|sp=us}} of this small comet. The flyby speed was 12.3&nbsp;km/s. The spacecraft employed the same suite of three science instruments—two telescopes and an infrared spectrometer—that the Deep Impact spacecraft used during its prime mission to guide an impactor into comet Tempel 1 in July 2005 and observe the results.<ref name="A'Hearn2011">{{Cite journal | last1 = A'Hearn | first1 = M. F. |author-link1=Michael A'Hearn | last2 = Belton | first2 = M. J. S. | last3 = Delamere | first3 = W. A. | last4 = Feaga | first4 = L. M. | last5 = Hampton | first5 = D. | last6 = Kissel | first6 = J. | last7 = Klaasen | first7 = K. P. | last8 = McFadden | first8 = L. A. | last9 = Meech | first9 = K. J. | last10 = Melosh | first10 = H. J. | last11 = Schultz | first11 = P. H. | last12 = Sunshine | first12 = J. M. | last13 = Thomas | first13 = P. C. | last14 = Veverka | first14 = J. | last15 = Wellnitz | first15 = D. D. | last16 = Yeomans | first16 = D. K. | last17 = Besse | first17 = S. | last18 = Bodewits | first18 = D. | last19 = Bowling | first19 = T. J. | last20 = Carcich | first20 = B. T. | last21 = Collins | first21 = S. M. | last22 = Farnham | first22 = T. L. | last23 = Groussin | first23 = O. | last24 = Hermalyn | first24 = B. | last25 = Kelley | first25 = M. S. | last26 = Kelley | first26 = M. S. | last27 = Li | first27 = J. -Y. | last28 = Lindler | first28 = D. J. | last29 = Lisse | first29 = C. M. | last30 = McLaughlin | first30 = S. A. | title = EPOXI at Comet Hartley 2 | journal = Science | volume = 332 | issue = 6036 | pages = 1396–1400 | year = 2011 | pmid = 21680835 | doi = 10.1126/science.1204054|bibcode = 2011Sci...332.1396A | s2cid = 6218729 }}</ref>

Early results of the observations show that the comet is powered by [[dry ice]], not water vapor as was previously thought. The images were clear enough for scientists to link jets of dust and gas with specific surface features.<ref name="A'Hearn2011"/>

"When comet Boethin could not be located, we went to our backup, which is every bit as interesting but about two years farther down the road," said Tom Duxbury, EPOXI project manager at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena, California. "Hartley 2 is scientifically just as interesting as comet Boethin because both have relatively small, active nuclei," said [[Michael A'Hearn]], principal investigator for EPOXI at the University of Maryland, College Park.<ref name="aHern"/>

===Sundry opportunities===
In November 2010, EPOXI was used to make some test-training deep sky observations, using the MRI camera that is optimised for cometary imagery. Images were made of the [[Dumbbell Nebula|Dumbbell Nebula (M27)]], the [[Veil Nebula|Veil Nebula (NGC6960)]] and the [[Whirlpool Galaxy|Whirlpool Galaxy (M51a)]].<ref>[http://stardustnext.jpl.nasa.gov/mission/deepSkyObservation.html Deep Sky Feasibility Demonstration] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120513165535/http://stardustnext.jpl.nasa.gov/mission/deepSkyObservation.html |date=2012-05-13 }}, [[NASA]], accessed 7 December 2011</ref>
